NeXTdimension: let the i860 run faster than 33 MHz

PREVIOUS_I860_SPEED=N gives the i860 N times the cycles a 33 MHz one has
in emulated time (the budget ND_VBL_Handler hands out every half frame),
as far as the host keeps up; "max" removes the limit.  The default, 1, is
as before.  Without a thread of its own (bI860Thread off), the i860's
cycles scale the same way, "max" as 64.

PREVIOUS_I860_STATS=1 prints the cycles a second the i860 ran, every 5
seconds of host time, to see what the host keeps up with.

--- src/dimension/dimension.cpp
+++ src/dimension/dimension.cpp
@@ -5,7 +5,9 @@
   or at your option any later version. Read the file gpl.txt for details.
 */
 
+#include <limits.h>
 #include <stdlib.h>
+#include <string.h>
 
 #include "main.h"
 #include "event.h"
@@ -314,7 +316,27 @@
 
     return i860.handle_msgs(msg);
 }
+
+/*
+ * PREVIOUS_I860_SPEED=N gives the i860 N times the cycles a 33 MHz one
+ * has in emulated time, as far as the host keeps up; "max" puts no limit
+ * on it.  By default (1) it gets 33 million a second.
+ */
+int nd_i860_speed(void) {
+    static int speed = -1;
 
+    if (speed < 0) {
+        const char* s = getenv("PREVIOUS_I860_SPEED");
+
+        speed = 1;
+        if (s && !strcmp(s, "max"))
+            speed = 0;
+        else if (s && atoi(s) > 0)
+            speed = atoi(s) < 1000 ? atoi(s) : 1000;
+    }
+    return speed;
+}
+
